WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.200
Every programmer has a brilliant engineering partner they trust.

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.700
Codex, Grok, Pi, or Claude Code.

00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:13.567
An idea becomes something you can build together.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.533
Once you have your own multi-agent workflow, the real problem becomes management.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.133
Who is progressing, who is blocked, and who takes the next step?

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.167
Herdr is the manager for those brilliant individual contributors.

00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:35.067
It brings workspaces, terminals, and agent status together.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.333
But the weekend is here. You should not have to sit at your computer watching progress.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.333
Who manages the manager?

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.967
Hermes on Herdr gives that job to a dedicated, trusted Hermes profile on this machine.

00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:54.733
Your M2: a manager for the manager.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.167
This dedicated Hermes has full Herdr control.

00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:06.700
It can inspect status, coordinate work, and carry your instructions to the agents doing it.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.100
When enabled, it starts with Herdr and stops with its session, inside a real pane.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:17.267
A pause stays paused until you explicitly resume.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:28.000
Open the monitor TUI: your dedicated instance, multiple profiles, CPU, and memory in view.

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:33.233
Check in remotely and asynchronously through your configured Hermes messaging platform.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.267
Keep the machine and Herdr online, and step away.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.967
Let the system manage the genius. Keep the weekend for yourself.

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:44.500
Hermes on Herdr. By Hexly AI.
